To make `useEffect` act like `componentDidMount`, you need to ensure that the function you pass to `useEffect` only runs once when the component mounts. This can be achieved by passing an empty dependency array `[]` as the second argument to `useEffect`. This way, the effect runs only after the first render, mimicking the behavior of `componentDidMount`. 

Here's how you can modify your `useEffect`:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  sendEnhancedTrackEvent(enhanceableData);
}, []); // The empty array ensures this runs only once when the component mounts
```

In your original attempt, you accidentally triggered an infinite loop because you didn't provide the dependency array, causing the effect to run on every render. Whenever you call a function that updates state inside `useEffect`, and you don't provide a dependency array, it will keep re-running on each render, leading to the infinite loop issue. This is a common pitfall when using hooks like `useEffect` in React [SO-60540843]. 

Additionally, your current implementation, which attempts to return a function from `useEffect`, is actually used for cleanup operations—in this case, it would not prevent the function from running on subsequent renders. If you solely want to run a side effect when the component mounts, just call the function directly inside `useEffect` and ensure you provide the empty dependency array. 

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  // This runs once on mount
  sendEnhancedTrackEvent(enhanceableData);
}, []); // No dependencies means it runs only after the first render
```

Make sure to also check that `sendEnhancedTrackEvent` and `enhanceableData` do not change, either by ensuring they are defined properly or are stable references, which will prevent any unnecessary reruns when using `useEffect`.

