To ensure that all edit text fields and the button remain visible when the virtual keyboard is displayed, you can wrap your layout in a `ScrollView`, which allows for scrolling when elements are obscured by the keyboard. In your case, the layout already uses a `ScrollView`, which is a good start. 

Here’s how to improve your setup to make sure all elements are visible:

1. **Set `android:fillViewport="true"` on the `ScrollView`**: This attribute ensures that the `ScrollView` will stretch its content to fill the available space, which can help in scenarios where the keyboard pops up, pushing content upwards.

2. **Use `ViewTreeObserver.OnGlobalLayoutListener`**: By implementing this listener, you can adjust the layout programmatically when the keyboard appears or disappears. Here's an outline of how that implementation looks:

    ```java
    public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity implements ViewTreeObserver.OnGlobalLayoutListener {

        private ScrollView scrollView;
        private RelativeLayout activity_main_RL;

        @Override
        protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
            super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
            setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

            scrollView = findViewById(R.id.login_form);
            activity_main_RL = findViewById(R.id.email_login_form);

            // Add the global layout listener
            activity_main_RL.getViewTreeObserver().addOnGlobalLayoutListener(this);
        }

        @Override
        public void onGlobalLayout() {
            Rect r = new Rect();
            getWindow().getDecorView().getWindowVisibleDisplayFrame(r);
            int heightDiff = getResources().getDisplayMetrics().heightPixels - (r.bottom - r.top);
            if (heightDiff > 200) { // if more than 200 dp, it's probably a keyboard...
                scrollView.smoothScrollTo(0, (int) (scrollView.getY() + yourTargetView.getHeight()));
            }
        }
    }
    ```

3. **Define proper layout for the `ScrollView`**: Make sure your layout proportions are defined correctly in XML so items are organized vertically. You essentially want your edit texts, buttons, and other interactive elements to still be scrollable when the keyboard is active. 

Here’s how your `ScrollView` should look:

```xml
<ScrollView
    android:id="@+id/login_form"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:fillViewport="true">
    <!-- Your existing layout inside -->
</ScrollView>
```

This structure ensures that, when a user clicks on an editText, the view will automatically adjust to show all fields and the button while the keyboard is open, addressing your requirement for good visibility during user input choices. By using `ScrollView` effectively, you keep elements visible and interactable even at smaller screen sizes, thus improving the usability of your login interface. 

Using approaches like these can help create a smoother user experience on mobile devices by avoiding the situation where only a portion of the form fields is visible, especially when the keyboard is raised.

